<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schutterij</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as schutterijen, schuttersgilde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A schutterij was a voluntary city guard or citizen militia of the medieval and early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, meant to defend a town against attack and to act in case of revolt or fire. Its members, grouped by district and by weapon, bought their own arms and kept the night watch at the gates; its officers were wealthy citizens appointed by the magistrates, and in the north after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> they had to belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a>. The ruling regenten were members, and the guards' group portraits, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>'s Night Watch of 1642, rank among the grandest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-golden-age">Dutch Golden Age</a> painting. Inactive by the 18th century, the schutterijen were abolished in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schutterij.jpg" alt="Schutterij" width="330" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rembrandt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Nightwatch_by_Rembrandt_-_Rijksmuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Members trained on grounds called the Doelen, usually beside a large hall where they met and where their group portraits hung; in bad weather they might train inside a church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Archers</a> and kloveniers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> bearers, formed separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, many under Saint Sebastian, Saint Anthony, Saint George or Adrian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, whose altars stood in local churches where the dues were paid. The ideal was three guardsmen for every hundred inhabitants. Two men guarded each district at night in shifts from 22:00 to 02:00 and from 02:00 to 06:00, and the company paraded once a month.</p><p>Not everyone served. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a> were excluded from the 16th to the 18th century and paid a double tax instead, and Roman Catholics were allowed only in the lower ranks. City servants such as the minister, physician, teacher and sexton, and the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, were exempt, while the beer and peat bearers served as the town's firefighters. A few years as an officer were often a step towards other public posts in the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the altars were removed and dues were paid at the city hall; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of St. Adriaen and St. Joris kept their old rules, with banquets and collections for sick members and widows. From 1581 the schutterijen were officially barred from influencing city politics. In 1748 the Doelisten asked stadtholder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-prince-of-orange">William IV, Prince of Orange</a>, to let the middle class appoint the officers, and he refused. In 1783 the Patriots tried to revive the guards or to found free corps open to all, with elected officers. The system outlasted the Batavian Revolution and French rule until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands">William I of the Netherlands</a> set up professional police forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Group portraits were usually painted when a leading officer died, retired or moved away, each sitter paying according to his place in the picture, which could cause quarrels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a>, Hendrik Gerritsz Pot and Caesar van Everdingen were members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> that commissioned them, and Bartholomeus van der Helst launched his career with his first such commission in 1639. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> works are in the Amsterdams Historisch Museum, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> in the Frans Hals Museum.</p><p>Ceremonial schutterijen that keep the old traditions survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, many of them in the Catholic regions. More than 160 from Belgian and Dutch Limburg compete each year at the Oud Limburgs Schuttersfeest, whose winner organises the next festival.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schutterij” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
